WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Let’s talk about what are probably the biggest protests happening anywhere in the world. 

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.480
Farmers in India have been demonstrating almost non-stop for months.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:22.200
They say new laws to improve agriculture are going to help big business but hurt small farmers.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:30.880
The protests even got Greta Thunberg and Rihanna chiming in — and lots of Indian media telling them to butt out.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:39.080
So what is it about these laws that farmers don’t like? 

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.960
Why does the Indian government think the protesters have it all wrong? 

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.160
And why is this about so much more than just farming?

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.800
How do we feed everyone, protect farming jobs and save the planet all at the same time?

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.680
It’s something most countries are trying to figure out.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.800
A lot of it comes down to choosing what kind of system they want. 

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:11.800
In some places the farming industry is driven by business. In others like the European Union governments subsidise agriculture.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.320
That’s the juncture India’s at right now.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:21.360
For a long time farming there has relied on subsidies and a whole lot of regulation.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:28.960
It's one way of supporting the nearly 1.4 billion people there given that about half of them work in agriculture. 

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.800
But in India farming’s been in trouble for a while.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.200
It only accounts for one-sixth of India's GDP.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:43.600
There are issues around land ownership, irrigation, corruption and unpredictable weather because of global warming.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.760
And all that's caused a lot of suffering.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.360
So something had to change. The government and the farmers both agree on that.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.800
What they can’t agree on is how.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.800
Last year the government brought in three laws to modernise the agriculture industry.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:17.320
One of the changes farmers are talking about relates to who they do business with.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:24.080
Most farmers have relied on state-controlled wholesale markets called “mandis”, where they are guaranteed a minimum price for their produce.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:27.280
The mandis then sell to buyers like supermarkets.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.520
But under the new laws buyers can avoid the mandis and do business directly with the farmers.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:37.200
Plenty of economists and the ruling BJP party say that’s an obvious win.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:56.320
But farmers and their unions are worried about being taken advantage of, that the mandi markets will eventually disappear, even though the government says that won’t happen.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:02.240
And that big corporations will demand lower prices from small farmers who have little bargaining power.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.640
There’s so much more to these laws and farmers have a bunch of other concerns.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.840
A lot of it boils down to a lack of trust.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:30.440
It hasn’t helped that the government rushed these laws through parliament last year without any real discussion. 

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.400
That’s what the farmers and opposition parties say.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.800
So they want the laws gone. And they’re sending a message.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:48.640
A convoy of tractors from India’s northern states descended on the capital but they were blocked at the city’s gates. 

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.360
So they set up three main campsites — and they’re huge.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.720
Hundreds of thousands of people have been living there for weeks.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.600
And things have gotten pretty heated.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:28.280
On India’s Republic Day protesters breached the walls of the Red Fort, one of the city’s main monuments, and police confronted them there.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:39.760
Police have also been closing in on the farmers’ campsites using rows of barricades and barbed wire.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:49.640
But they’ve gone further than that.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:10.480
And then there’s the media wars, especially after Western celebrities started commenting on the crackdown. 

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.760
And Indian celebrities hit back.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:33.800
So this has snowballed into something bigger than just farming.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:40.120
They’re talking about people’s right to dissent, police violence, even the whole idea of democracy in India.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.080
And some say party politics are making it all worse.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:49.600
Critics of Prime Minister Narendra Modi think he’s creating business opportunities for his rich friends.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.080
Then you’ve got the ruling party and state media blaming the protests on opposition groups.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:06.120
They point to these protesters waving a Sikh flag as proof that separatists from the north are involved too.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:37.840
Those laws are meant to reform the industry. But they now seem to stand in the way of any progress at all.

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:41.680
What’s missing is a whole lot of trust — but they have a starting point. 

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:45.880
Pretty much everyone agrees that farming in India needs fixing.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:54.880
So hopefully that was a useful primer. There is so much more in these farm laws and plenty of politics playing out. 

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:59.680
As always check out our website aljazeera.com for the latest news and I’ll see you next week.

